    The slope captures how steeply the reference model's probability mass decays beyond high-probability tokens, characterizing the shape of the distribution tail.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Surface features.}
Two features capture lexical diversity without requiring a reference model:
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{TTR}: type-token ratio (number of unique word types divided by total tokens);
    \item \textbf{hapax\_ratio}: proportion of words appearing exactly once (\emph{hapax legomena}).
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Feature complementarity.}
The grouping reflects an empirical pattern we term \emph{differential depth response}: central-tendency and diversity features (entropy\_mean, TTR, hapax\_ratio) show substantial monotonic drift with generation depth ($|\rho| = 0.60$--$0.63$); entropy\_min and entropy\_std show moderate drift ($|\rho| = 0.17$--$0.41$); and shape features (entropy\_max, tail\_decay) remain near-stationary ($|\rho| < 0.10$).
This differential behavior, consistent with recursive generation shifting the location of the NLL distribution while preserving distributional shape, motivates combining both feature types.
As shown in \S\ref{sec:results}, the full 7-feature set significantly outperforms either group alone.

\subsection{Evaluation Protocol}
\label{sec:evaluation}

\paragraph{Cross-validation.}
To evaluate generalization to unseen generators, we use \textbf{Leave-One-Generator-Out (LOGO)} cross-validation: in each fold, one of the eight generators is held out for testing and the remaining seven provide training data.
We additionally report \textbf{Leave-One-Family-Out (LOFO)} results, where an entire model family (e.g., all Qwen models) is held out, testing generalization across architectural lineages.

\paragraph{Classifier.}
We use XGBoost \cite{xgboost} throughout ($n\_\text{estimators}{=}200$, $\max\_\text{depth}{=}6$, learning rate $0.1$), with identical hyperparameters across all methods in Table~\ref{tab:baselines}.
LOGO F1 is stable across random seeds (std\,$<$\,0.001 over 5 seeds).
Alternative classifiers and ordinal-specific methods (CORAL \cite{coral_ordinal}, Frank-Hall \cite{frank_hall_ordinal}) yield comparable performance (Appendix~\ref{app:classifier}), confirming that discriminative power resides in the features.

\paragraph{Metrics and statistical testing.}
The primary metric is \textbf{macro-averaged F1} over the three depth classes.
We report 95\% confidence intervals via percentile bootstrap ($n{=}1{,}000$), McNemar tests for pairwise comparisons, and paired bootstrap for baseline comparisons.
All $p$-values are two-sided; Bonferroni correction is applied where multiple comparisons arise.
Feature--depth correlations (\S\ref{sec:results-differential}) use class-balanced subsamples to control for depth-class imbalance.

\paragraph{Baselines.}
We compare the 7-feature ensemble against four single-score detection methods repurposed as ordinal depth predictors: Avg-LogProb, FastDetectGPT \cite{fast_detectgpt}, Binoculars \cite{binoculars}, and LD-Score \cite{ld_score}.
Each baseline score is used as a univariate feature in the same XGBoost framework under LOGO cross-validation, ensuring a controlled comparison.

\paragraph{Length confound control.}
Since TTR and hapax\_ratio are mechanically length-dependent, we verify depth signal is not a length artifact via length-matched subsampling that equalizes length distributions across depth classes (\S\ref{sec:results-classification}; Appendix~\ref{app:length}).

