\caption{8-generator LOGO macro-F1 for the 7-feature ensemble (bootstrap 95\% CIs, $n$\,=\,1000). Binary F1 (gen-1 vs.\ gen-2+) is the primary metric. Note: 3-class F1 may carry upward bias from gen-0 seed sharing across generators; binary F1 is leak-free. Cross-model chains (Table~\ref{tab:cross-ref}) use sample-level CV without this issue.}
\label{tab:logo-results}
\end{table}

\subsection{Differential Depth Response}
\label{sec:results-differential}

Features exhibit \emph{differential depth response} (DDR): central-tendency and diversity measures drift monotonically with depth, while shape measures remain near-stationary, consistent with recursive generation inducing a location shift in the NLL distribution while preserving its functional form.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig1_differential_response.pdf}
\caption{Differential depth response: Spearman correlations between individual features and generation depth. Central-tendency and diversity features (hapax ratio, TTR, entropy mean) show substantial monotonic drift; entropy std ($|\rho|{=}0.17$) occupies an intermediate position; shape features (entropy max, tail decay) remain near-stationary ($|\rho| < 0.10$).}
\label{fig:feature-drift}
\end{figure}

Pooled class-balanced Spearman correlations with depth confirm the split (Figure~\ref{fig:feature-drift}):
drifting features show substantial monotonic trends (hapax ratio $\rho = -0.63$, TTR $\rho = -0.61$, entropy mean $\rho = -0.60$),
while shape features remain near-stationary (tail decay $|\rho| = 0.04$); entropy std occupies an intermediate position ($|\rho| = 0.17$).

\paragraph{Distributional shift quantification.}
Two-sample Kolmogorov--Smirnov tests between gen-1 and gen-2+ distributions isolate the recursive depth effect (excluding the initial human$\rightarrow$LLM transition): drifting features exhibit large distributional shifts (entropy mean KS\,=\,0.537, TTR\,=\,0.517, hapax ratio\,=\,0.516; all $p \approx 0$), while shape features show order-of-magnitude smaller shifts (entropy max KS\,=\,0.043, tail decay\,=\,0.052).
All seven features reach statistical significance after Bonferroni correction, but shape-feature KS statistics are ${\sim}10\times$ smaller than drifting features, supporting the ``approximately stationary'' characterization.
Entropy std occupies an intermediate position (KS\,=\,0.285), consistent with its borderline rank correlation.
Levene tests for variance homogeneity reject equal variance for all seven features ($p < 10^{-10}$), indicating that recursive generation alters spread as well as location, though the magnitude of variance change is substantially smaller for shape features (coefficient of variation shift $<$3\% for entropy max vs.\ $>$15\% for hapax ratio).

\paragraph{Feature complementarity.}
Under 10-fold stratified CV, the full 7-feature set (F1\,=\,0.773) outperforms surface-only (F1\,=\,0.688) and reference-only (F1\,=\,0.713; McNemar $p \approx 0$; Table~\ref{tab:ablation}).
Reference-only yields higher binary F1 (0.833 vs.\ 0.695) since surface features introduce noise at the gen-1/gen-2+ boundary, but surface features are essential for 3-class gen-0 discrimination (Full 0.773 vs.\ Reference-only 0.713).
Permutation importance: hapax ratio (0.35), entropy mean (0.23), TTR (0.18); leave-one-feature-out confirms 6 of 7 features contribute positively (Appendix~\ref{app:lofo}).

\paragraph{Cross-family reference models.}
Alternative references (OLMo-2-1B, Qwen3-0.6B) achieve comparable LOGO F1 (spread 0.013; Table~\ref{tab:cross-ref}), with consistently negative entropy drift, confirming DDR is a property of recursive generation.

\paragraph{Temperature robustness.}
The method operates reliably within $t \in [0.5, 0.9]$: F1 varies by at most 1\,pp from the default $t{=}0.7$ across this range (Appendix~\ref{app:sampling-sensitivity}).
At $t{=}1.0$, performance degrades by ${\sim}6$\,pp as the primary depth signal collapses: entropy mean's Spearman correlation with depth drops from $\rho = -0.60$ to $\rho = -0.002$, indicating that high-temperature sampling injects sufficient token-level randomness to mask the systematic location shift that DDR exploits.

